| 1 | K | Media all around us. *Where do we meet media in our day, and what do its parts tell us?* | 18 | ma-k-read; ma-k-society | MA:Re7.1.Ka; MA:Re7.1.Kb; MA:Cn11.1.Ka; MA:Cn11.1.Kb | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through the first viewfinder pictures (review). Works: Eadweard Muybridge, The Horse in Motion (United States, 1878, the Library of Congress, public domain), a row of photographs of one running horse; studio portraits by Seydou KeA_ta (Bamako, Mali, about 1949 to 1962), described and linked to a holding museum's page, never copied; Hildegard Westerkamp, Kits Beach Soundwalk (Vancouver, Canada, 1989; born 1946 in Germany; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a sound piece made on a walk by the sea, described and linked to the artist's own page; the class's own viewfinder drawings and sound map of media in the school (a sign, the bell, a poster, a screen), with no faces | MA:Re7.1.Ka: notice the parts of a media piece (pictures, sounds, words; still or moving) and tell what it seems to say, on Muybridge's row and on school signs. MA:Re7.1.Kb: notice and share how two pieces give different experiences (looking at the horse photographs against listening to the soundwalk with eyes closed). MA:Cn11.1.Ka: with help, share where media shows up in the child's day (the bell, a sign, a song, a picture book read aloud), on a media-hunt walk in the school, with a story character's day offered beside the child's own and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.Kb: with help, use media tools and spaces safely and politely: the viewfinder and any shared device held in two hands and set down on the table, a turn waited for, asking before pointing at anyone, no picture of a face. Technique: framing with a card viewfinder (hold, look, choose what is inside, draw it); a listening walk with a picture sound map. Words: media, part, message, sound, frame, safe. Showing: the class's "media in our school" wall of viewfinder drawings and sound maps. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.Ka, a talk item: on an unseen piece (an open-licensed photograph or a Curio Class sign card) the child points to two parts and says what it seems to say; MA:Re7.1.Kb, a talk item: an unseen still picture and an unseen short sound (a Curio Class original pair), how each feels to look at or listen to; MA:Cn11.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept "media in a day" drawing: one place media shows up in a day (the child's own or the story character's, marked the same), told to the teacher as the child draws; MA:Cn11.1.Kb, a table item: from the tray the child picks the viewfinder or the device and shows its safe, polite handling (two hands, set down, asks first, no face) and says one rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: making a piece that says something (unit 3), roles (unit 2). Tools: card viewfinder template, sound-map card, media-hunt picture cards, the media rules card, step cards, work cards, all Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed card. Free path: viewfinders cut by an adult from cereal-box card. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit needs the safe, polite tool habits and the looking and listening routine (the media-ethics-check and look-describe-interpret models start here). Looking and listening open the year so a child meets media before making it. Four ids, two techniques and the set-up and hand-back routine learned for the first time | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on device set-up and hand-back minutes inside a 40-minute session | Hazards and controls: media-hunt and listening walks: the class stays together and in sight; any shared device held in two hands, set on the table, wiped and handed back by the device card; screen breaks; viewfinder card edges checked smooth by an adult. No child is photographed or drawn from the viewfinder without a yes; the hunt points at things, never faces. KeA_ta photographed the people of Bamako in his studio: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and published accounts of the studio first; children look at the portraits and never remake them with classmates' faces. Home media vary: "a story character's day" is offered beside "your day" and marked the same. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | K | Playing with light and shadow. *How do media makers find an idea, and what can a new tool teach us?* | 22 | ma-k-ideas; ma-k-craft | MA:Cr1.1.K; MA:Cr2.1.K; MA:Pr5.1.Ka; MA:Pr5.1.Kb; MA:Pr5.1.Kc | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced in each opener (review). Works: Lotte Reiniger, The Adventures of Prince Achmed (Germany, 1926; cut-paper silhouette animation; public domain in the United States since 2022, the unit plan checks the copy's page), a short part with the flying horse; Norman McLaren and Evelyn Lambart, Begone Dull Care (Canada, 1949, the National Film Board of Canada's free page at nfb.ca), paint and scratches made straight on film to music, linked, never copied | MA:Cr1.1.K: play with tools (paper cutouts, a flashlight and a sheet or paper screen, crayon on a long strip, a free drawing program where the school has one) until an idea for a piece turns up, then tell it. MA:Cr2.1.K: with help, shape the idea into a simple plan or model: a three-box storyboard card (first, next, last) or a cutout on a stick as a model. MA:Pr5.1.Ka: show basic skills with a partner: using the tool, making a choice, working together on a shadow piece. MA:Pr5.1.Kb: show a creative skill inside the piece, such as performing: moving the puppet or making the sound (behind the screen, so no face is seen). MA:Pr5.1.Kc: try a new tool and tell what it taught (the light nearer makes the shadow bigger). Technique: silhouette cutouts on a stick and shadow play with a flashlight; the three-box storyboard. Words: idea, play, plan, tool, shadow, together. Showing: the partner shadow pieces, shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.K, a guide look-for on the kept idea page: two or more tries with a tool and an idea told (the teacher writes the told idea on the page); MA:Cr2.1.K, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard card or model: first, next and last pictured; MA:Pr5.1.Ka, a look-for at the shadow screening (check day 1): the child handles the light or the puppet, makes one choice and works with the partner; MA:Pr5.1.Kb, a look-for at the same screening: the child performs one part (moves the puppet or makes the sound); MA:Pr5.1.Kc, a talk item (check day 2): one thing the flashlight or a cutout taught. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: changing a finished piece (unit 3), naming roles in a showing (unit 4). Tools: step cards, the storyboard card, the light-and-shadow card, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box card, a borrowed flashlight or a window's daylight on a paper screen. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 1 because shadow play needs the tool rules (two hands, set down, the light never in eyes) and play with a tool needs the habit of looking at what a piece shows. Before unit 3 because the kamishibai piece is made from a storyboard plan with the tool skills built here. The storyboard and production-roles models start here. Longest kindergarten unit: five ids, two techniques and the first partner work | Candidate: a doubled parent-and-child version of the partner shadow piece for the home room | Hazards and controls: flashlights low-power, never shone in eyes, no flashing on and off; the screen on a steady stand; blunt-tip scissors, card cut by an adult. Partner work: a child who finds sharing hard works the light while the partner moves the puppet, by turns. Prince Achmed draws on the Arabian Nights tales and shows its people and places in a 1920s European picture of "the East": the unit shows only the flying horse and the silhouette craft, and the sensitivity agent reads the copy's page and a film archive's notes first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | K | Make it, change it. *What can our picture-card story say, and what happens when we change one thing?* | 10 | ma-k-make; ma-k-judge | MA:Cr3.1.Ka; MA:Cr3.1.Kb; MA:Re8.1.K; MA:Re9.1.K | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through the kamishibai's setting (review). Works: kamishibai, the Japanese paper theater in which a street storyteller slid picture cards through a small wooden stage and told the story (Japan, from about 1930), a children's story set from a museum's or library's open collection (the unit plan picks it and records its rights line); Anna Atkins, Photographs of British Algae: Cyanotype Impressions (England, 1843 to 1853, public domain, a library's open digital copy), the same kind of plant shown again and again, each print laid out a little differently | MA:Cr3.1.Ka: create and capture a three-card picture story that says something (a pet, the weather, an invented animal), each card drawn from what the viewfinder frames. MA:Cr3.1.Kb: change one thing (a color, what is in the frame, the order of two cards) and share both versions with the class. MA:Re8.1.K: with help, share what you notice about different media pieces. MA:Re9.1.K: share what looks appealing in a piece and one thing you might change (the critique card: I notice, I like, I would change). Technique: the cereal-box kamishibai stage, cards slid in order and told aloud by the child or a partner. Words: make, change, card, notice, like, version. Showing: both versions told to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept three cards: three cards in order that say one thing; MA:Cr3.1.Kb, a guide look-for on the kept before-and-after card: the first card and the changed card side by side, the change told to the teacher at the showing and noted on the record sheet; MA:Re8.1.K, a talk item: one thing noticed about each of two unseen pieces (an open-licensed photograph and a Curio Class card); MA:Re9.1.K, a talk item: on an unseen piece, what looks appealing and one change. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pattern and repetition (grade 1). Tools: the kamishibai stage template, picture cards, the critique card, the before-and-after card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a cereal box and scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After unit 2 because the story is made from a storyboard plan and with the tool habits; judging needs unit 1's noticing of parts. Before unit 4 because the screening joins a piece the child has already made and changed. The revision-log and critique-protocol models start here. Short because the plan, the framing and the tool skills are already built | Candidate: a second picture-card form from Africa or the Americas beside kamishibai, for range | Hazards and controls: cereal boxes cut by an adult; blunt-tip scissors and glue sticks; hands washed. Kamishibai was also used for wartime messages in 1930s and 1940s Japan: the unit picks a children's story set, and the sensitivity agent reads the holding collection's page and a Japanese museum's or cultural body's page on kamishibai first; children make their own stories in the form and copy no set's pictures. Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | K | Our class screening. *How can we join pictures and a dance into one show, and how did showing it feel?* | 10 | ma-k-present; ma-k-sources | MA:Pr4.1.K; MA:Pr6.1.Ka; MA:Pr6.1.Kb; MA:Cn10.1.Ka; MA:Cn10.1.Kb | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced on the strip (review). Works: Annabelle Serpentine Dance (United States, 1895, filmed at Thomas Edison's studio and colored by hand, the Library of Congress, public domain), a dance joined with film and color; McLaren and Lambart's Begone Dull Care from unit 2, revisited: paint and music joined | MA:Pr4.1.K: with help, join two art forms into one piece: a paper film strip (a long strip of drawn pictures pulled through a box window) with a class movement made to it. MA:Pr6.1.Ka: with help, name the roles (puller, mover, sound maker, usher) and the place where the piece is shown, on the screening-plan card. MA:Pr6.1.Kb: with help, share reactions to the screening (how showing felt, what the audience did). MA:Cn10.1.Ka: use own experiences and choices in the piece (the strip shows something the child did or likes, or a story character's, marked the same). MA:Cn10.1.Kb: share a memorable media moment (a song, a picture book read aloud, a picture), with a class-shared moment (unit 2's shadow screening) offered beside it and marked the same. Technique: the pull-through film strip in a box window, pulled at a steady pace to a movement or a sound. Words: join, dance, screen, role, show, feel. Showing: the class screening for families, with each family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.K, a look-for at the screening (check day 1): pictures and movement joined in one piece; MA:Pr6.1.Ka, a kept card made on the unit's last making day: the screening-plan card with the roles and the place pictured, scored with the piece; MA:Cn10.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept strip: a picture from the child's own experience or choice (or the story character's, marked the same); MA:Pr6.1.Kb, a talk item (check day 2): how showing felt and one thing the audience did; MA:Cn10.1.Kb, a talk item (check day 2): one memorable media moment, the child's own or the class-shared one, marked the same. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: showing conditions (grade 1). Tools: the film-strip box template, the screening-plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a cereal box and taped paper strips. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in the year because the screening joins the year's framing, plan and change skills and ends the year. The year-end check sits before it (week 27), outside the core checks of weeks 29 to 32; this unit's screening in weeks 28 to 32 is its own assessment. The screening-plan and source-log models start here | Candidate: the found-sound track written as the full equal of the movement version | Leave-out: a school may leave out the movement (STANDARD.md 5); a found-sound track joined to the strip keeps every id met. The movement is the class's own, never an imitation of a tradition's dance. Hazards and controls: a clear floor with room to move arms out, no jumping from heights, no partner contact; strips pulled by hand, no sharp box edges. Memorable media moments are invited, never required: no child is asked what the family watches, and home media is never judged. No child is filmed; a family's yes before any showing outside class, under the child's chosen name. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 1 | Media that links people. *How do pictures and films link us to people we know, and how do two pieces feel different?* | 18 | ma-g1-read; ma-g1-society | MA:Re7.1.1a; MA:Re7.1.1b; MA:Cn11.1.1a; MA:Cn11.1.1b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Horace Poolaw (Kiowa, 1906 to 1984), photographs of Kiowa family and community life in and around Anadarko, Oklahoma (about 1925 to 1955), from the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian (its 2014 exhibition "For a Love of His People"), described and linked; Louis LumiA"re, Repas de bAcbAc (Baby's Meal) (France, 1895, public domain), a family at breakfast filmed: a still and a moving picture of family life | Retrieval in day 2's opener: noticing parts and messages (ma-k-read) and safe, polite tool use (ma-k-society). MA:Re7.1.1a: name what media pieces contain (people, a place, things, a sound) and the messages they carry. MA:Re7.1.1b: with help, name how two pieces create different experiences (a still photograph of a family against a short film of one). MA:Cn11.1.1a: discuss media in daily life and how it links people you know (a picture sent to a grandparent, a call, a class newsletter), with a story character's or an invented family's link offered beside the child's own and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.1b: use tools and spaces by the rules, with safety and fair play: the class media rules the children help write (ask before a picture of anyone; a classmate only with their yes and the family's; the device shared by the turn card). Technique: far, middle and close shots with the viewfinder, drawn in three frames. Words: contents, message, far, close, link, fair. Showing: a "media links us" wall of three-frame drawings of places and objects. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.1a, a talk item: on an unseen open-licensed photograph, what it contains and its message; MA:Re7.1.1b, a talk item: an unseen still and an unseen short film clip of one subject, how each feels different; MA:Cn11.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept "media links us" drawing: a media link between people (the child's own, invented or a story character's, marked the same), told to the teacher; MA:Cn11.1.1b, a table item: with the viewfinder or the device from the tray, the child shows the turn card and the ask-first rule in action and names one class rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: media in cultural life (grade 2). Tools: the three-frame card, the class rules card, work cards, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: viewfinders from year K, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the tools and the rules after the summer and moves the looking routine from parts to contents and messages. Both kindergarten models (look-describe-interpret, media-ethics-check) are retrieved here, where they are first needed this year. The far, middle and close shots taught here are what unit 6's sketches in order draw | Candidate: a third photograph of family life by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked, for range | Poolaw photographed his own Kiowa family and community: the sensitivity agent studies the National Museum of the American Indian's pages and a Kiowa-run source first; the unit picks everyday-life photographs, not ceremonies, and no child imitates dress or regalia. Families vary: "people you know" includes classmates, neighbors and an invented family. Hazards and controls: shared devices by the turn card, wiped and handed back; screen breaks; no picture of a child without a yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 1 | Pictures in order. *How do media makers plan a piece in pictures, and what jobs does a team need?* | 22 | ma-g1-ideas; ma-g1-craft | MA:Cr1.1.1; MA:Cr2.1.1; MA:Pr5.1.1a; MA:Pr5.1.1b; MA:Pr5.1.1c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: A%mile Cohl, Fantasmagorie (France, 1908, public domain), a film made from hundreds of line drawings in order; Reiniger's Prince Achmed from year K, revisited: the same moving-picture idea made with cut paper instead of line | Retrieval in day 2's opener: play and a first plan (ma-k-ideas) and a tool used with a partner (ma-k-craft). MA:Cr1.1.1: show an idea in three sketches in order and a small model. MA:Cr2.1.1: with help, build a mockup of one named idea: a four-to-eight page flip book. MA:Pr5.1.1a: describe and show a role (planner, drawer, page holder, sound helper) and its skills (planning, tool steps, teamwork). MA:Pr5.1.1b: show basic creativity by changing up a technique (the flip book drawn again bigger, in color, or with a faster move). MA:Pr5.1.1c: try varied uses of a tool (the flip book flipped slow and fast, the viewfinder close and far) and show classmates what you found. Technique: the flip book, small changes from page to page. Words: sketch, order, mockup, flip book, role, try. Showing: team flip books shown to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept sketch page: three sketches in order and a small model; MA:Cr2.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept flip-book mockup: built from the named idea on the sketch page; MA:Pr5.1.1a, a talk item: with the role card, the child names the role and shows and tells one of its steps; MA:Pr5.1.1b, a guide look-for on the two kept flip books: the first version and the changed one; MA:Pr5.1.1c, a table item: with a flip book or viewfinder from the tray, the child shows one varied use and tells what it found. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: brainstorming many ideas (grade 2). Tools: the sketch-in-order card, flip-book template, role cards, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book. Free path: scrap paper stapled or clipped. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 5 because sketches in order draw its far, middle and close shots. Before unit 7, whose pattern and repetition need pages in order. Longest grade 1 unit: five ids, the flip book and the first named roles | Candidate: an animated work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked, for range | Team work: every role has a seated, no-drawing and no-speaking version; a child may work beside the team with a doubled task. Prince Achmed: as unit 2, only the flying horse and the craft are shown. Staples or clips by an adult; blunt scissors. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 1 | Patterns that repeat. *How do patterns and repeats help a media piece, and which part works best for its viewers?* | 10 | ma-g1-make; ma-g1-judge | MA:Cr3.1.1a; MA:Cr3.1.1b; MA:Re8.1.1; MA:Re9.1.1 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: J.D. 'Okhai Ojeikere, the Hairstyles series of photographs (Nigeria, from 1968), described and linked to a holding museum's page; Muybridge's The Horse in Motion from year K, revisited: a repeat of frames | MA:Cr3.1.1a: make and record content (frames drawn through the viewfinder, or photographs of objects on a shared device), assemble them into a six-frame strip, and spot pattern and repetition (a repeated frame, close, far, close). MA:Cr3.1.1b: try a change, notice what it does, and use it to finish. MA:Re8.1.1: with help, identify what different pieces mean. MA:Re9.1.1: name the part of a piece that works best and a possible change, thinking of its viewers. Technique: a six-frame sequence with a repeat, laid out as a strip. Words: pattern, repeat, record, effect, meaning, viewer. Showing: the strips shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept strip: six frames assembled with a repeat or pattern, which the child has circled on the last making day; MA:Cr3.1.1b, a guide look-for on the kept before-and-after card: one change tried and used to finish, and what it did, dictated or drawn; MA:Re8.1.1, a talk item: what an unseen piece means; MA:Re9.1.1, a talk item: the part of an unseen piece that works best and a change for its viewers. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: placing content to draw attention (grade 2). Tools: the six-frame strip card, the before-and-after card, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil frames on scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After unit 6 because a strip with a pattern needs pages in order and a team's roles; meaning and viewers build on year K's noticing. Short because the order, framing and roles are already built | Candidate: a pattern-based piece by a living maker from Asia or the Americas, described and linked | Ojeikere photographed Nigerian women's hairstyles: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and published accounts of the series first; the unit studies pattern in the photographs, and no child is asked about, photographed for or compared by their own hair or body (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
