em: two unseen pieces, how each shapes a different experience; MA:Cn11.1.2a, a guide look-for on the kept poster and its card: the message named and one place in daily or community life where it would be seen, written or dictated; MA:Cn11.1.2b, a table item: at the tray the child shows the group rules in action (the device turn, the credit line on the group poster pointed to) and names a safety rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: values and online behavior (grade 3). Tools: the poster layout card, the credit card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper and glue sticks. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 2 because it reopens the tools and rules after the summer, with a group project's fair-play and credit rules shown first; the message idea grows from grade 1's contents and messages. The poster's single message is what unit 11's placement for attention builds on | Candidate: a media message piece by a maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked | Red Star is ApsA�alooke and made these photographs to answer how old pictures showed her people: the teacher says this plainly, and the sensitivity agent studies the artist's own published words and an ApsA�alooke-run source first. Her dress is studied by looking only, as she presents it; no child dresses as, poses as or plays a Native person (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). WPA posters: the unit plan's picks are checked for the era's stereotypes. "Cultural life" tasks never ask a child to explain their own family's culture. Hazards and controls: blunt scissors, glue sticks, devices by the turn card. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
