-for on the kept storyboard and mockup of the chosen idea; MA:Pr5.1.2a, a talk item: with the role card, the child names the role and the skill it used; MA:Pr5.1.2b, a guide look-for on the kept first-try and second-try frames side by side: tried again with one change; MA:Pr5.1.2c, a table item: with a cutout and the device or paper frames from the tray, the child shows one named method (move, capture, move) and names it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: testing a plan with classmates (grade 3). Tools: the brainstorm card, storyboard card, role cards, the sound-score card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book. Free path: paper cutouts, paper frames, voice and found objects for sound. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 9 because a team's fair roles and credit were set there; many ideas grow from grade 1's single idea in order. Before unit 11, whose whole project combines the contents recorded and shaped here. Longest grade 2 unit: five ids and two recording methods | Candidate: a living stop-motion maker from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Printed Rainbow is about an old woman living alone; the teacher names it gently as a story about imagination, and the sensitivity agent reads the maker's or festival's page first. Leave-out: acting out ideas may be left out; quick sketches keep every id met. Hazards and controls: no flashing in any stop-motion piece (no fast color flicker); screen breaks; sound at low volume; devices by the turn card. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
