Each check day: the class makes at tables with paper and crayon while the teacher hears six groups of five for about
four minutes each; set-up and clean-up fit the rest of the 40 minutes, and no device is out but the one on the
teacher's table. Every child's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are
band course days (year 1 starts on day 65, year 2 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

No media arts row of grades K to 2 carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv), so this course waits
on no other course and no other course waits on it. The contacts below are practiced, not owned, each with its "if
later" line; all are core courses (lesson 32).

### Mathematics, kindergarten to grade 2 (same year)

| Used here | Contact | If math has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Units 2 and 3 (year K): first, next and last on the storyboard; three picture cards | Counting small sets, in math kindergarten unit 1 (days 1 to 18) | Order is said as first, next and last by pointing; no counting beyond three is asked |
| Unit 7 (year 1): a repeat in a six-frame strip | Patterns are not a math standard in K to 2; counting to six is kindergarten math | The repeat is found by looking (the same picture again), never by counting rules |

No unit asks for measuring, fractions, time on a clock, symmetry or scale (lesson 13).

### English language arts, kindergarten to grade 2 (same year)

Talk about media pieces and the critique practice collaborative conversation (SL.K.1, SL.1.1, SL.2.1), and telling a
picture-card tale practices retelling with key details: practiced, not owned. If ELA has not yet taught the
turn-taking rules, the critique card shows them in pictures (look, listen, one at a time) and the teacher models them
on the first critique day.

### Social studies, kindergarten to grade 2

Unit 9's "life of a time, a place and a people" (year 2) sits near social studies grade 2's unit 6, "Family stories
and culture". No lean either way: the art unit teaches the setting it uses itself, on work cards (bar criterion 7),
and never asks a child to explain their own family's culture.

### Computer science (not a core course)

STANDARD.md 6: media arts teaches the one idea of privacy or credit its unit needs in a short step and does not wait
for computer science. Here: asking before a picture and no faces (units 1, 5 and 9) and naming a source (units 9 and
12). Computer science goes deeper on attribution in grade 8 (computer-science:g8-attribution); its K to 2 courses
are not core, so nothing here assumes them.

## For the lead

1. **Media arts connecting ids swapped (settled).** Checked at source (the California standards page for
   MA:Cn10 grade 3): anchor 10 is the student's own experience and anchor 11 society, culture and history, as in
   every other discipline. The words-file lines for MA:Cn10.1.* and MA:Cn11.1.* were swapped, every ma-*-sources
   row now carries the Cn10 ids and every ma-*-society row the Cn11 ids, and this frame's id labels changed to match
   (the pairs of rows stay in their units).
2. **Media arts weekly time figure.** STANDARD.md 3.4 has no media arts figure; the visual and media arts
   association's elementary checklist (the same file the visual arts pilot read for its 50 minutes) is read at source
   for its media arts lines, then the Pending note in the Days bullet is replaced with the figure and the gaps on both
   paths.
3. **After-core check weeks.** This frame's checks are in week 21 (mid-year) and week 27 (year-end) in every year,
   off every core check week. Week 21 holds no other check today; week 27 shares with theater K to 2 in all three
   grades, and with computer science K, health and library K (grade K), computer science 1 and health (grade 1), and
   ELD, health and physical education (grade 2). Left for the
   lead's one calendar pass. The once-a-week path's respond sessions are in weeks 22 and 28.
4. **Arts disciplines in the stagger check** (as the pilot's item 2): two arts courses in one week are never
   flagged while the check keys on subject.
5. **Paper path meets the capture and tool ids (item-level, decided).** I read "media tool" as anything that
   frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a device-free class meets MA:Cr3.1.Ka, MA:Pr5.1.*
   and MA:Pr5.1.2c (recording) in full on paper (a drawn frame, a sound score). If the lead or Cory wants recording
   ids met only on a device, the course needs one shared device per group of five, which is a cost question.
6. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $35 (year K), $30 (year 1), $40 (year 2) a class of
   30, all optional beside a free path. Pick: the same ceiling as the visual arts pilot's pick, $75 a class a year,
   with every unit's free path kept.
