data/progressions/arts.csv:400:ma,K,ma-k-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.K; MA:Cr2.1.K,,ma-g1-ideas,,storyboard,idea; play; plan; model; tell; tool,,"Play with a free drawing program, a school-approved camera and paper cutouts until an idea for a media piece turns up, say the idea aloud, and with help make a simple plan or model. What changes: first ideas found by play, and a first plan made with an adult's help. Assessed by a storyboard check: the teacher marks each child's idea pages as met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:401:ma,K,ma-k-make,MA:Cr3.1.Ka; MA:Cr3.1.Kb,,ma-g1-make,,revision-log,make; capture; change; share; content; look,,"Make a first media piece, such as a photo, a sound or a drawing, that says something, then change one thing about it and show the class both versions. What changes: a first finished piece, and one change made on purpose. Assessed by a finished piece and a short talk scored met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:402:ma,K,ma-k-craft,MA:Pr5.1.Ka; MA:Pr5.1.Kb; MA:Pr5.1.Kc,,ma-g1-craft,,production-roles,tool; choose; work together; try; learn; how it works,,"Use a media tool, make choices and share the work with a partner, take a small part in a piece such as performing in it, and tell what a new tool taught you. What changes: first hands on a tool and first shared work. Assessed by a practice check of tool use and teamwork marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: devices are school-approved and shared. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:403:ma,K,ma-k-present,MA:Pr4.1.K; MA:Pr6.1.Ka; MA:Pr6.1.Kb,,ma-g1-present,,screening-plan,mix; dance; video; show; role; feel,,"With help, join two art forms, such as dance and video, into one media piece, name who did what and where it is shown, and say how the showing felt. What changes: a first joined piece and a first showing, with help. Assessed by a showing and a short talk marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:404:ma,K,ma-k-read,MA:Re7.1.Ka; MA:Re7.1.Kb,,ma-g1-read,,look-describe-interpret,notice; parts; message; experience; tell; media,,"Look at and listen to short media pieces, point to their parts, and say what each seems to say and how it feels to watch. What changes: first noticing of parts and messages. Assessed by a look-and-tell response marked met, partly or not yet."
data/progressions/arts.csv:405:ma,K,ma-k-judge,MA:Re8.1.K; MA:Re9.1.K,,ma-g1-judge,,critique-protocol,notice; share; appealing; change; different; media,,"With help, tell the class what you notice in a media piece, which part appeals to you and one thing you would change. What changes: first noticing, liking and wishing aloud. Assessed by a talk about a media piece marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:406:ma,K,ma-k-society,MA:Cn11.1.Ka; MA:Cn11.1.Kb,,ma-g1-society,,media-ethics-check,daily life; media; safe; polite; tools; share,,"With help, share where media shows up in your day, and practice using media tools and spaces safely and politely. What changes: first talk about media in daily life, and first safe, polite tool habits. Assessed by a talk and a safe-use check marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: Students never post or share media of others online. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:407:ma,K,ma-k-sources,MA:Cn10.1.Ka; MA:Cn10.1.Kb,,ma-g1-sources,,source-log,own experience; choice; media; share; memory; make,,"Put your own experiences and choices into a media piece, and tell about a media moment you remember. What changes: your own experience becomes the content of a piece. Assessed by a made piece and a short talk marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: use the child's own drawings and recordings and class-made media, or media that is free to use."
data/progressions/arts.csv:408:ma,1,ma-g1-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.1; MA:Cr2.1.1,ma-k-ideas,ma-g2-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,sketch; mockup; model; idea; plan; help,,"Show an idea for a media piece in three sketches in order and a small model, then with help build a mockup of one named idea. What changes: ideas are now drawn in order and modeled, where kindergarten only played and told. Assessed by a storyboard check: the teacher marks each child's idea pages as met, partly or not yet."
data/progressions/arts.csv:409:ma,1,ma-g1-make,MA:Cr3.1.1a; MA:Cr3.1.1b,ma-k-make,ma-g2-make,revision-log,revision-log,record; assemble; pattern; repetition; change; effect,,"Make and record content, put the parts together and point to a pattern or repeat in it, then try one change, notice what it did and use it to finish. What changes: the child names a pattern in the piece and uses a change to finish, not only makes one. Assessed by a finished piece and a short talk in which the child points to the pattern, scored met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:410:ma,1,ma-g1-craft,MA:Pr5.1.1a; MA:Pr5.1.1b; MA:Pr5.1.1c,ma-k-craft,ma-g2-craft,production-roles,production-roles,role; plan; teamwork; technique; try; show,,"Take a named role such as planner or camera helper, change up one technique, and show classmates what a tool can do. What changes: named roles, and a technique varied on purpose. Assessed by a practice check of tool use and teamwork marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: devices are school-approved and shared. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:411:ma,1,ma-g1-present,MA:Pr4.1.1; MA:Pr6.1.1a; MA:Pr6.1.1b,ma-k-present,ma-g2-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,blend; subject; tale; show; condition; task,,"Put a story or subject, a drawing and a sound into one piece, such as a picture-book tale, do one job when it is shown and tell how showing felt. What changes: subjects and media joined into one piece, with one job in the showing. Assessed by a showing and a short talk marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:412:ma,1,ma-g1-read,MA:Re7.1.1a; MA:Re7.1.1b,ma-k-read,ma-g2-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,contents; message; experience; name; help; different,,"Name what is in a media piece and what it says, and with help say how two pieces feel different to watch. What changes: names for contents, and a first comparison of how pieces feel. Assessed by a look-and-tell response marked met, partly or not yet."
data/progressions/arts.csv:413:ma,1,ma-g1-judge,MA:Re8.1.1; MA:Re9.1.1,ma-k-judge,ma-g2-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,meaning; identify; effective; change; viewer; help,,"With help, say what a media piece means, point to the part that works best and suggest one change for its viewers. What changes: the child says what a piece means and thinks about its viewers, not only what they notice. Assessed by a talk about a media piece marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:414:ma,1,ma-g1-society,MA:Cn11.1.1a; MA:Cn11.1.1b,ma-k-society,ma-g2-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,daily life; people you know; safe; fair play; rules; tools,,"Talk about how media connects you to people you know, and use media tools and spaces fairly and by the rules. What changes: media is seen as a link between people, and fair play and rules are named. Assessed by a talk and a safe-use check marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:415:ma,1,ma-g1-sources,MA:Cn10.1.1a; MA:Cn10.1.1b,ma-k-sources,ma-g2-sources,source-log,source-log,interest; example; experience; share; meaningful; make,,"Draw on your own interests and an example you can name when making media, and share a media experience that mattered to you. What changes: an example is named as a source, beyond the child's own memory. Assessed by a made piece and a short talk marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: use the child's own and class-made media, or media that is free to use, and name where an example came from."
data/progressions/arts.csv:416:ma,2,ma-g2-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.2; MA:Cr2.1.2,ma-g1-ideas,ma-g3-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,brainstorm; improvise; choose; mockup; sketch; many ideas,,"Brainstorm and act out many ideas for one piece, choose one, and make a sketch and a mockup of it. What changes: many ideas and then one chosen, where grade 1 showed a single idea. Assessed by a storyboard check: the teacher marks each child's idea pages as met, partly or not yet."
data/progressions/arts.csv:417:ma,2,ma-g2-make,MA:Cr3.1.2a; MA:Cr3.1.2b,ma-g1-make,ma-g3-make,revision-log,revision-log,combine; whole project; position; attention; change; describe,,"Combine several pieces of content into one whole project, placing them to draw the eye, then try a change near the end and describe how it read. What changes: several contents are combined into a whole and the result of a change is described, where grade 1 spotted a pattern. Assessed by a finished piece and a short talk scored met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:418:ma,2,ma-g2-craft,MA:Pr5.1.2a; MA:Pr5.1.2b; MA:Pr5.1.2c,ma-g1-craft,ma-g3-craft,production-roles,production-roles,role; tool; play; try again; record; shape,,"Take a role that uses art, design, tech or people skills, experiment by playing and trying again, and show named ways of recording and shaping media with a tool. What changes: trying again after a first attempt, and tool methods called by name. Assessed by a practice check of tool use and teamwork marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:419:ma,2,ma-g2-present,MA:Pr4.1.2; MA:Pr6.1.2a; MA:Pr6.1.2b,ma-g1-present,ma-g3-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,narrated; animation; unified; conditions; carry out; results,,"Blend a subject, art and media into one piece, such as a narrated animation, say where and how it will be shown, do a task in the showing and describe how it went. What changes: one unified piece, with the showing conditions named. Assessed by a showing and a short talk in which the child names the showing conditions, marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:420:ma,2,ma-g2-read,MA:Re7.1.2a; MA:Re7.1.2b,ma-g1-read,ma-g3-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,describe; contents; message; shape; experience; name,,"Describe what a media piece holds and what it says, and how two pieces shape different experiences for the same viewer. What changes: how a piece shapes an experience is described, not only what it holds. Assessed by a look-and-tell response marked met, partly or not yet."
data/progressions/arts.csv:421:ma,2,ma-g2-judge,MA:Re8.1.2; MA:Re9.1.2,ma-g1-judge,ma-g3-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,purpose; context; improve; work; discuss; meaning,,"Say what a media piece is for and what it means, think about where it is seen, and discuss how well it works and what would help. What changes: purpose and setting join meaning, and ways to improve are discussed. Assessed by a talk about a media piece marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:422:ma,2,ma-g2-society,MA:Cn11.1.2a; MA:Cn11.1.2b,ma-g1-society,ma-g3-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,culture; message; safe; fair play; rules; tools,,"Talk about how media ideas fit daily and cultural life and what a media message is, and keep using tools and spaces safely and fairly. What changes: culture and the idea of a media message arrive. Assessed by a talk and a safe-use check marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:423:ma,2,ma-g2-sources,MA:Cn10.1.2a; MA:Cn10.1.2b,ma-g1-sources,ma-g3-sources,source-log,source-log,know; like; read; example; experience; meant,,"Draw on what you know, like and have read, plus an example, to make media, and talk about a media experience and what it meant. What changes: reading and liking join personal experience as sources, and meaning is discussed. Assessed by a made piece and a short talk marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:424:ma,3,ma-g3-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.3; MA:Cr2.1.3,ma-g2-ideas,ma-g4-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,develop; method; material; test; plan; share,,"Develop several ideas with different tools or materials, then share and test the plan, sketch or model with classmates before making the piece. What changes: ideas are tested with classmates before making, where grade 2 only chose one. Assessed by a planning portfolio entry in which the child shows what a classmate's test changed, scored on a three-line rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:425:ma,3,ma-g3-make,MA:Cr3.1.3a; MA:Cr3.1.3b,ma-g2-make,ma-g4-make,revision-log,revision-log,order; unified; movement; force; stress; effect,,"Put varied content in a purposeful order, using movement or force, then stress one element and analyze how that changes the effect. What changes: order serves a purpose, and a stressed element is analyzed, not only a change described, where grade 2 combined content into a whole. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a three-line rubric for purpose, effect and change. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:426:ma,3,ma-g3-craft,MA:Pr5.1.3a; MA:Pr5.1.3b; MA:Pr5.1.3c,ma-g2-craft,ma-g4-craft,production-roles,production-roles,compose; tool handling; plan; new content; standard way; answer,,"Take a group role such as composing or tool handling, invent new content for a project, and use tools in their standard ways to build a piece. What changes: group roles with growing skill, and standard tool use. Assessed by a production role record and a tool-use task scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:427:ma,3,ma-g3-present,MA:Pr4.1.3; MA:Pr6.1.3a; MA:Pr6.1.3b,ma-g2-present,ma-g4-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,blend; form; share; distribute; role; improve,,"Blend forms and content into one piece, describe the showing conditions, take a role and steps for sharing it, and suggest ways to show it better. What changes: a role and steps for sharing, and a suggested improvement. Assessed by a finished mixed piece, a showing plan with each step named and a short talk with one suggested improvement, scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:428:ma,3,ma-g3-read,MA:Re7.1.3a; MA:Re7.1.3b,ma-g2-read,ma-g4-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,parts; build; technique; style; audience; describe,,"Take apart how the parts of a piece build its message, and how a technique or style steers the audience. What changes: how parts build messages and how styles steer an audience are explained step by step, where grade 2 described what a piece holds. Assessed by a look-and-describe response scored on a three-line rubric for naming, evidence and a reason."
data/progressions/arts.csv:429:ma,3,ma-g3-judge,MA:Re8.1.3; MA:Re9.1.3,ma-g2-judge,ma-g4-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,judge; setting; criteria; evaluate; improve; purpose,ela:SL.3.1 (class talk about what pieces mean and whether they work),"Judge what purposes a piece carries, describe its setting, and name basic criteria to weigh it and suggest improvements. What changes: the first named criteria, and a judgment given with them. Assessed by a first critique using the critique protocol, scored on a three-line rubric for criteria, evidence and a next step. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:430:ma,3,ma-g3-society,MA:Cn11.1.3a; MA:Cn11.1.3b,ma-g2-society,ma-g4-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,values; online; behavior; safety; fair play; rules,,"Explain how media touches daily life, including the values it shapes and how people act online, and examine tools and spaces for safe, fair use. What changes: media is seen as shaping values and online behavior, where grade 2 linked media to culture. Assessed by a short explanation in words or media and a safe-use check scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:431:ma,3,ma-g3-sources,MA:Cn10.1.3a; MA:Cn10.1.3b,ma-g2-sources,ma-g4-sources,source-log,source-log,interest; outside source; example; popular media; meaning; culture,,"Make a short media piece from the student's own interests and an outside source, and show with an example how popular media shapes what something means. What changes: outside sources and popular media as a shaper of culture, where grade 2 drew on what the child knew and had read. Assessed by a made piece with a source log, scored on a three-line rubric for sources, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:432:ma,4,ma-g4-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.4; MA:Cr2.1.4,ma-g3-ideas,ma-g5-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,goal; original; mockup; combine; presentation; test,,"Set an original goal for a piece with sketches and mockups, then discuss, test and combine plans while keeping the goal and the showing in mind. What changes: an original goal is set first, and plans are combined, not only tested. Assessed by a planning portfolio entry that states the goal and shows two plans combined, scored on a three-line rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:433:ma,4,ma-g4-make,MA:Cr3.1.4a; MA:Cr3.1.4b,ma-g3-make,ma-g5-make,revision-log,revision-log,arrange; purpose; balance; contrast; refine; effect,,"Arrange varied content to carry a purpose with balance or contrast, then refine it until it produces the effect you intended. What changes: content is arranged for a stated purpose and refined toward an intended effect, where grade 3 analyzed the effect of stress. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a three-line rubric for purpose, effect and change. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:434:ma,4,ma-g4-craft,MA:Pr5.1.4a; MA:Pr5.1.4b; MA:Pr5.1.4c,ma-g3-craft,ma-g5-craft,production-roles,production-roles,technique; equipment; production; design thinking; usual way; new way,,"Rotate production roles in a team project, solve a media problem with design steps (define, sketch, test), and try a tool in its usual way and a new one. What changes: design thinking and a new use of a tool, where grade 3 used tools in standard ways. Assessed by a production role record and a tool-use task scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:435:ma,4,ma-g4-present,MA:Pr4.1.4; MA:Pr6.1.4a; MA:Pr6.1.4b,ma-g3-present,ma-g5-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,coordinate; form; public; explain; role; improve,,"Combine content from a school subject, an art form and media in one piece, take a named job in a showing to an audience, and explain how it went. What changes: a public sharing is explained, beyond the class circle. Assessed by a finished mixed piece, a showing plan and a short talk explaining what to improve, scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:436:ma,4,ma-g4-read,MA:Re7.1.4a; MA:Re7.1.4b,ma-g3-read,ma-g5-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,explain; parts; message; technique; style; audience,,"Mark how the parts of a short piece build its message, then point to the techniques and styles that steer the audience. What changes: explaining why, where grade 3 described how. Assessed by a look-and-describe response scored on a three-line rubric for naming, evidence and a reason."
data/progressions/arts.csv:437:ma,4,ma-g4-judge,MA:Re8.1.4; MA:Re9.1.4,ma-g3-judge,ma-g5-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,reaction; explain; production step; criteria; improve; context,,"Give a reasoned reaction to different pieces that weighs purpose and context, and use basic criteria to judge and improve pieces and production steps. What changes: a reasoned reaction, and criteria applied to production steps, not only to finished pieces. Assessed by a critique that also judges a production step, scored on a three-line rubric for criteria, evidence and a next step. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:438:ma,4,ma-g4-society,MA:Cn11.1.4a; MA:Cn11.1.4b,ma-g3-society,ma-g5-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,culture; fantasy; reality; ethics; fair play; tools,,"Make a short piece in words or media on how media ties to culture, such as fantasy against reality, and check the class's tools and spaces for fair and ethical use. What changes: fantasy is told from reality and ethics joins safety, where grade 3 named values and online behavior. Assessed by a short explanation in words or media and a safe-use check scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:439:ma,4,ma-g4-sources,MA:Cn10.1.4a; MA:Cn10.1.4b,ma-g3-sources,ma-g5-sources,source-log,source-log,research; cultural knowledge; online; meaning; experience; inform,,"Research the student's interests and cultural knowledge to shape a media piece, and show how media makes meaning online. What changes: research and cultural knowledge inform the piece, and online spaces are shown to make meaning, where grade 3 used outside sources. Assessed by a made piece with a source log, scored on a three-line rubric for sources, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:440:ma,5,ma-g5-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.5; MA:Cr2.1.5,ma-g4-ideas,ma-g6-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,imagine; proposal; audience; model; experience; test,,"Draw new ideas from your own experience or from others' work, then develop and test a proposal and model for a stated audience. What changes: ideas are drawn from others' work, and a proposal is built for an audience. Assessed by a planning portfolio entry that names an audience and shows a test, scored on a three-line rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:441:ma,5,ma-g5-make,MA:Cr3.1.5a; MA:Cr3.1.5b,ma-g4-make,ma-g6-make,revision-log,revision-log,join; component; emphasis; clarity; change; refine,,"Join content parts so they express a meaning, using emphasis, then decide what could change for clearer communication and refine for clarity. What changes: the student decides what to change for clarity before refining, not after, where grade 4 refined toward an intended effect. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a three-line rubric for purpose, effect and change. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:442:ma,5,ma-g5-craft,MA:Pr5.1.5a; MA:Pr5.1.5b; MA:Pr5.1.5c,ma-g4-craft,ma-g6-craft,production-roles,production-roles,role; fundamentals; convention; bend; ordinary; experimental,,"Practice varied roles, bend a usual convention to solve a problem in a project, and compare ordinary and experimental uses of a tool. What changes: a convention bent on purpose and experimental tool use compared with ordinary, where grade 4 used tools in usual and new ways. Assessed by a production role record and a tool-use task scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:443:ma,5,ma-g5-present,MA:Pr4.1.5; MA:Pr6.1.5a; MA:Pr6.1.5b,ma-g4-present,ma-g6-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,newscast; format; compare; role; results; improve,,"Make a piece that blends kinds of content, such as a newscast, weigh what two showing formats offer, take a role and compare results and improvements. What changes: two formats are weighed against each other, in a piece built from mixed content. Assessed by a finished newscast-style piece, a showing plan comparing two formats and a short talk, scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:444:ma,5,ma-g5-read,MA:Re7.1.5a; MA:Re7.1.5b,ma-g4-read,ma-g6-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,tell apart; message; meaning; technique; style; steer,,"Compare how the parts of two pieces build message and meaning, and how their techniques and styles steer the audience differently. What changes: two pieces are told apart side by side, where grade 4 explained one. Assessed by a look-and-describe response scored on a three-line rubric for naming, evidence and a reason."
data/progressions/arts.csv:445:ma,5,ma-g5-judge,MA:Re8.1.5; MA:Re9.1.5,ma-g4-judge,ma-g6-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,compare; personal; group; criteria; feedback; aim,,"Compare your reading of a piece with the group's, weighing its aim and setting, and set criteria to evaluate pieces and production steps with kind, useful feedback. What changes: personal and group readings are compared, and the criteria are set by the student. Assessed by a critique using the protocol in which the student sets the criteria and gives feedback that names a look-for, scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:446:ma,5,ma-g5-society,MA:Cn11.1.5a; MA:Cn11.1.5b,ma-g4-society,ma-g6-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,advertising; community; history; ethics; media literacy; tools,,"Make a short media piece on how media shows up in the student's community, ads and their history included, and keep a class checklist for ethical, media-literate use of tools and spaces. What changes: advertising and history join the study and media literacy joins ethics, where grade 4 separated fantasy from reality. Assessed by a short explanation in words or media and a safe-use check scored on a three-line rubric. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:447:ma,5,ma-g5-sources,MA:Cn10.1.5a; MA:Cn10.1.5b,ma-g4-sources,ma-g6-sources,source-log,source-log,gather; source; news; community event; cultural experience; knowledge,,"Make a media piece from the student's knowledge, experiences and outside sources, and show how news coverage or a community event shapes people's cultural experience. What changes: the student gathers the sources, and news and community events show how media shapes culture, where grade 4 researched an interest. Assessed by a made piece with a source log, scored on a three-line rubric for sources, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:448:ma,6,ma-g6-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.6; MA:Cr2.1.6,ma-g5-ideas,ma-g7-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,creative method; aim; organize; production step; critique; purpose,,"Use a chosen creative method to set several aims for a media project, then organize plans, mockups and production steps and critique them against the purpose. What changes: the student picks the method and critiques the plan against a purpose. Assessed by a planning portfolio where each plan is critiqued against its stated purpose, scored on a rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:449:ma,6,ma-g6-make,MA:Cr3.1.6a; MA:Cr3.1.6b,ma-g5-make,ma-g7-make,revision-log,revision-log,approach; point of view; purposeful; element; audience; refine,,"Try several approaches to make content with a point of view, and judge how changing elements could reach the intended effect before refining for the audience. What changes: several approaches are tried and compared before the audience refinement, where grade 5 decided changes for clarity. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:450:ma,6,ma-g6-craft,MA:Pr5.1.6a; MA:Pr5.1.6b; MA:Pr5.1.6c,ma-g5-craft,ma-g7-craft,production-roles,production-roles,assigned role; constraint; flexible; solution; ordinary; experimental,,"Perform an assigned production role, test constraints to find solutions, and use tools in both ordinary and experimental ways. What changes: roles are assigned and constraints are deliberately tested, where grade 5 practiced varied roles. Assessed by a role record, a tool-use task and a self-check scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:451:ma,6,ma-g6-present,MA:Pr4.1.6; MA:Pr6.1.6a; MA:Pr6.1.6b,ma-g5-present,ma-g7-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,message; format; analyze; distribute; task; results,,"Blend several forms in one piece to back its core message, analyze where and how it could be shown, do varied tasks to present and share it, and analyze how the showing went. What changes: formats are analyzed against a core message, and a written review replaces a talk. Assessed by a finished piece, a showing plan and a written review that ties each format to the core message, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:452:ma,6,ma-g6-read,MA:Re7.1.6a; MA:Re7.1.6b,ma-g5-read,ma-g7-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,analyze; parts; meaning; technique; style; audience,,"Write an analysis of how a piece's parts build its meaning, with a reason from the piece, and how its techniques and styles steer an audience. What changes: from naming parts to explaining how a choice steers a viewer, with a reason from the piece. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:453:ma,6,ma-g6-judge,MA:Re8.1.6; MA:Re9.1.6,ma-g5-judge,ma-g7-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,criteria given; analyze; intend; particular criteria; feedback; judge,ela:SL.6.1 (class talk about what pieces mean and whether they work),"Critique pieces' intent with given criteria, then choose criteria of the student's own to judge a piece and its production steps, with feedback. What changes: criteria are given first and then chosen by the student. Assessed by a written critique that uses given criteria first and chosen criteria second, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:454:ma,6,ma-g6-society,MA:Cn11.1.6a; MA:Cn11.1.6b,ma-g5-society,ma-g7-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,identity; history; entertainment; fair use; copyright; ethics,,"Make a short piece on how media ties to identity, history, entertainment and community, and credit every source in it, checked against the class copyright and ethics card. What changes: identity joins the investigation and copyright becomes a rule for the student's own media use, where grade 5 stopped at advertising and history. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:455:ma,6,ma-g6-sources,MA:Cn10.1.6a; MA:Cn10.1.6b,ma-g5-sources,ma-g7-sources,source-log,source-log,judge; resource; research; historical event; new meaning; inner,,"Find, weigh and draw on sources, including research, to make media, and show how media gives events new meaning. What changes: sources are weighed for quality and history becomes the setting, where grade 5 only gathered them. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:456:ma,7,ma-g7-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.7; MA:Cr2.1.7,ma-g6-ideas,ma-g8-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,prototype; inventive; draft; resources; expressive; assess,,"Use an inventive process such as prototyping to produce varied ideas, then draft and judge plans for the expressive aim and the resources on hand. What changes: a prototype is built and plans are judged against resources. Assessed by a planning portfolio with a prototype and a resource check, scored on a rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:457:ma,7,ma-g7-make,MA:Cr3.1.7a; MA:Cr3.1.7b,ma-g6-make,ma-g8-make,revision-log,revision-log,coordinate; merge; narrative structure; stress; audience; setting,,"Run the production steps to shape content into a story, then improve the piece by stressing the elements its audience and setting need. What changes: production steps are coordinated and a narrative structure is used, where grade 6 tried approaches for an audience. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:458:ma,7,ma-g7-craft,MA:Pr5.1.7a; MA:Pr5.1.7b; MA:Pr5.1.7c,ma-g6-craft,ma-g8-craft,production-roles,production-roles,role; organize; explore; adapt; purpose; method,,"Work across roles, solve problems creatively and organize the work, explore while building solutions, and adapt tools to a given purpose. What changes: tools are adapted to a given purpose and the work is organized, where grade 6 used them in ordinary and experimental ways. Assessed by a role record, a tool-use task and a self-check scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:459:ma,7,ma-g7-present,MA:Pr4.1.7; MA:Pr6.1.7a; MA:Pr6.1.7b,ma-g6-present,ma-g8-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,viewpoint; story; evaluate; format; result; growth,,"Merge content types into one work with a steady viewpoint and story, evaluate showing formats to choose one, and weigh results against your own growth. What changes: a consistent viewpoint, and a growth check on the student's own work. Assessed by a finished piece, a showing plan and a written review that includes the student's own growth, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:460:ma,7,ma-g7-read,MA:Re7.1.7a; MA:Re7.1.7b,ma-g6-read,ma-g8-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,compare; relate; personal taste; qualities; shape; experience,,"Compare the qualities of parts of pieces and how they relate, and analyze how technique and style join personal taste to shape an experience. What changes: personal taste is admitted into the analysis and weighed, where grade 6 analyzed meaning from the piece alone. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:461:ma,7,ma-g7-judge,MA:Re8.1.7; MA:Re9.1.7,ma-g6-judge,ma-g8-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,own criteria; analyze; mean; build; constructive; evaluate,,"Critique what pieces intend and mean with criteria the student built, and refine those criteria for judging a piece and its production steps. What changes: the student builds the criteria, rather than choosing among them. Assessed by a written critique that explains why each of the student's own criteria was chosen, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:462:ma,7,ma-g7-society,MA:Cn11.1.7a; MA:Cn11.1.7b,ma-g6-society,ma-g8-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,career; purpose; value; social platform; responsible; analyze,,"Make a short piece on what one kind of media is for and the values it carries (a career, a social platform), and write the class's rules for using media tools and spaces responsibly. What changes: careers and social platforms are examined for responsible use, where grade 6 studied identity and copyright. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:463:ma,7,ma-g7-sources,MA:Cn10.1.7a; MA:Cn10.1.7b,ma-g6-sources,ma-g8-sources,source-log,source-log,varied sources; strong example; learning; new knowledge; guide; explain,,"Find, weigh and use varied sources, strong model works among them, to guide a media piece, and explain how media builds new knowledge, for example in learning. What changes: strong example works guide the making and learning is the setting, where grade 6 used history as the setting. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:464:ma,8,ma-g8-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.8; MA:Cr2.1.8,ma-g7-ideas,ma-g9-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,many directions; workflow; prototype; intent; resources; setting,,"Use a method such as thinking in many directions to produce ideas, then structure a workflow and prototype and critique it for intent, resources and showing setting. What changes: a whole workflow is planned and tested, where grade 7 judged a plan. Assessed by a planning portfolio with a workflow and a critiqued prototype, scored on a rubric for range, testing and a stated aim."
data/progressions/arts.csv:465:ma,8,ma-g8-make,MA:Cr3.1.8a; MA:Cr3.1.8b,ma-g7-make,ma-g9-make,revision-log,revision-log,blend; style convention; unity; technical quality; polish; viewer,,"Blend content and style conventions into a unified piece through the production steps, then polish its technical quality for its purpose, viewers and setting. What changes: style conventions are blended on purpose, with technical polish, where grade 7 merged content for meaning. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:466:ma,8,ma-g8-craft,MA:Pr5.1.8a; MA:Pr5.1.8b; MA:Pr5.1.8c,ma-g7-craft,ma-g9-craft,production-roles,production-roles,range of skills; convention; problem; adapt; intent; content,,"Perform assigned roles across a range of skills, bend a convention to solve a named problem, and adapt tools, methods and content to your intent. What changes: content as well as tools is adapted to the student's intent, where grade 7 adapted tools to a given purpose. Assessed by a role record, a tool-use task and a self-check scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:467:ma,8,ma-g8-present,MA:Pr4.1.8; MA:Pr6.1.8a; MA:Pr6.1.8b,ma-g7-present,ma-g9-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,theme; format; context; distribute; improvement; effect,,"Merge forms into one work that carries a theme, design how it will be shown in several formats or contexts, and put improvements in place after evaluating. What changes: a theme is shown across more than one format, and improvements are put in place. Assessed by a finished piece, a multi-format showing plan and a written review that records an improvement actually made, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:468:ma,8,ma-g8-read,MA:Re7.1.8a; MA:Re7.1.8b,ma-g7-read,ma-g9-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,contrast; links; style; feel; intend; analyze,,"Compare the parts and styles inside two media pieces, and how their techniques steer what audiences feel. What changes: contrast across styles in a single analysis, where grade 7 compared parts and taste. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:469:ma,8,ma-g8-judge,MA:Re8.1.8; MA:Re9.1.8,ma-g7-judge,ma-g9-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,form; context; built criteria; goals; judge; production step,,"Critique intent and meaning across forms and settings, and judge a piece and its production steps with criteria the student built, weighing setting and goals. What changes: built criteria are weighed against the maker's goals and the context. Assessed by a written critique that weighs the maker's goals, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:470:ma,8,ma-g8-society,MA:Cn11.1.8a; MA:Cn11.1.8b,ma-g7-society,ma-g9-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,civic; environment; value; legal; virtual world; responsible,computer-science:g8-attribution (when credit is owed for copied or remixed media),"Make a piece that shows how media serves civic, environmental or community values, and use tools responsibly within legal and online limits. What changes: civic and environmental values and the legal setting of media tools, where grade 7 stopped at careers and platforms. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:471:ma,8,ma-g8-sources,MA:Cn10.1.8a; MA:Cn10.1.8b,ma-g7-sources,ma-g9-sources,source-log,source-log,social knowledge; model work; local; worldwide; cultural; create,,"Find, weigh and use cultural and social knowledge, research and model works to make a media piece, and show how media widens knowledge from local to worldwide events. What changes: cultural and social knowledge and model works guide the making, where grade 7 used example works as guidance alone. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
data/progressions/arts.csv:472:ma,9,ma-g9-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.I; MA:Cr2.1.I,ma-g8-ideas,ma-g10-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,idea method; goal; aesthetic standard; prototype; inspiration; setting,,"Use named methods to form many ideas and set goals, then apply a short list of aesthetic standards to refine plans and prototypes for their setting. What changes: named methods and written aesthetic standards, where grade 8 chose methods freely. Assessed by a planning portfolio and prototype scored on a rubric for range, research and a stated aim. Level 1 bridge: the level's first unit (unit 1, before this row's unit 3) teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id (at most 12 days in the full year, 6 on the 80-day short path, STANDARD.md 3.2)."
data/progressions/arts.csv:473:ma,9,ma-g9-make,MA:Cr3.1.Ia; MA:Cr3.1.Ib,ma-g8-make,ma-g10-make,revision-log,revision-log,organize; style; emphasis; tone; aesthetic quality; personal goal,,"Organize a piece's content and style with deliberate choices of emphasis or tone, and refine its look and sound to match the student's stated goals. What changes: choices are deliberate and tied to the student's own goals, where grade 8 blended conventions for set purposes. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:474:ma,9,ma-g9-craft,MA:Pr5.1.Ia; MA:Pr5.1.Ib; MA:Pr5.1.Ic,ma-g8-craft,ma-g10-craft,production-roles,production-roles,choose a role; risk; constraint; combine; innovative; intent,,"Choose and carry out a production role, take creative risks to meet a set limit or challenge, and combine usual and new tools to carry the intent. What changes: roles are chosen by the student and risk is taken on purpose, where grade 8 worked in assigned roles. Assessed by a production log and a tool-use task scored on a rubric for skill, risk and adapting. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:475:ma,9,ma-g9-present,MA:Pr4.1.I; MA:Pr6.1.Ia; MA:Pr6.1.Ib,ma-g8-present,ma-g10-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,audience reaction; collection; format; judge; act; community,,"Blend arts and media forms into one work planned for audience reaction, and design how a collection is shown and distributed for varied audiences. What changes: a collection, not a single piece, is designed for varied audiences. Assessed by a finished piece, a collection showing plan and a written review, scored on a rubric for message, format and improvement. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:476:ma,9,ma-g9-read,MA:Re7.1.Ia; MA:Re7.1.Ib,ma-g8-read,ma-g10-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,preference; artist choice; sense; audience; intent; analyze,,"Write an analysis of how parts, style and preferences show up in artists' choices, and how a piece steers experience through more than one sense. What changes: artists' choices are read and several senses are considered, where grade 8 contrasted styles. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:477:ma,9,ma-g9-judge,MA:Re8.1.I; MA:Re9.1.I,ma-g8-judge,ma-g10-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,received; cultural setting; key stage; identified criteria; aims; evaluate,,"Critique how pieces are meant and received in personal and cultural settings, and review the student's own work at key production stages against named criteria. What changes: evaluation at key stages of production, not only on the finished piece. Assessed by a written critique at two production stages, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:478:ma,9,ma-g9-society,MA:Cn11.1.Ia; MA:Cn11.1.Ib,ma-g8-society,ma-g10-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,power; equality; trend; identity; digital identity; career,,"Make a piece that shows how media ties to power, equality and identity, and weigh the legal, technical and career sides of media work, digital identity included. What changes: power, identity and digital identity enter the study, where grade 8 stopped at civic values. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:479:ma,9,ma-g9-sources,MA:Cn10.1.Ia; MA:Cn10.1.Ib,ma-g8-sources,ma-g10-sources,source-log,source-log,outside source; own source; weave; original; online learning; widen,,"Find, weigh and combine outside sources with the student's own ideas to shape an original piece, and show how media builds culture, for example through online learning. What changes: outside and own sources are woven into one original piece, where grade 8 used each separately. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:480:ma,10,ma-g10-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.II; MA:Cr2.1.II,ma-g9-ideas,ma-g11-ideas,storyboard,storyboard,strategy; personal aesthetic; original; limits; prototype; approach,,"Choose idea methods on purpose to sharpen goals and make an approach more original, then design a prototype in your own aesthetic within stated limits. What changes: methods are chosen strategically and the aesthetic is the student's own. Assessed by a planning portfolio and prototype that states the limits and the student's aesthetic, scored on a rubric for range, research and a stated aim. Level 2 opens with a review unit of at most 6 days that serves as placement."
data/progressions/arts.csv:481:ma,10,ma-g10-make,MA:Cr3.1.IIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIb,ma-g9-make,ma-g11-make,revision-log,revision-log,continuity; juxtaposition; convention; look; technical; chosen audience,,"Organize content and style with continuity or juxtaposition, and refine the look and technical parts so the work lands with chosen audiences. What changes: work is refined for the impact on a chosen audience, where level 1 honed quality toward personal goals. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:482:ma,10,ma-g10-craft,MA:Pr5.1.IIa; MA:Pr5.1.IIb; MA:Pr5.1.IIc,ma-g9-craft,ma-g11-craft,production-roles,production-roles,manage; produce; explore; failure; interactivity; style,,"Manage and produce a project with strong command of skills, keep exploring after early failures, and blend tools, styles and interactivity toward an expressive goal. What changes: managing a production and learning from failure on hard challenges, where level 1 chose roles and took risks. Assessed by a production log and a tool-use task scored on a rubric for skill, risk and adapting. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:483:ma,10,ma-g10-present,MA:Pr4.1.II; MA:Pr6.1.IIa; MA:Pr6.1.IIb,ma-g9-present,ma-g11-present,screening-plan,screening-plan,theme; style; curate; channel; judge; society,,"Merge arts, media and school-subject content under one theme, curate a collection for a wide audience and online channels, then judge and improve the showing. What changes: curated collections for mass and virtual audiences. Assessed by a finished piece, a curated collection plan for a virtual channel and a written review, scored on a rubric for message, format and improvement. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:484:ma,10,ma-g10-read,MA:Re7.1.IIa; MA:Re7.1.IIb,ma-g9-read,ma-g11-read,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,synthesize; impact; persuasion; multiple senses; feedback; analyze,,"Compare how parts relate across several pieces with feedback on audience impact, and how pieces use several senses to steer experience and persuade. What changes: persuasion and comparison across pieces, where level 1 analyzed single pieces and artists' choices. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:485:ma,10,ma-g10-judge,MA:Re8.1.II; MA:Re9.1.II,ma-g9-judge,ma-g11-judge,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,influence; historical; defended; systematic; critique; evaluation,,"Write an analysis of a media work's purpose, meaning and influence across personal, social, historical and cultural settings, and defend an evaluation through a structured critique. What changes: evaluations are defended against objections across settings. Assessed by a written critique that answers one objection, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
data/progressions/arts.csv:486:ma,10,ma-g10-society,MA:Cn11.1.IIa; MA:Cn11.1.IIb,ma-g9-society,ma-g11-society,media-ethics-check,media-ethics-check,commerce; propaganda; truth; legal; interactivity; systemic,,"Investigate how media ties to commerce, power, propaganda and truth, and work ethically within legal, technical and career limits, including audience interaction. What changes: commerce, propaganda and truth are examined, where level 1 studied power and identity. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:487:ma,10,ma-g10-sources,MA:Cn10.1.IIa; MA:Cn10.1.IIb,ma-g9-sources,ma-g11-sources,source-log,source-log,introspection; research; persuasive; combine; reflect; culture,,"Combine reflection on the student's own views with research to strengthen a persuasive piece, and use media to reflect on culture. What changes: persuasion is built on introspection and research, where level 1 wove sources into one piece. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
data/progressions/arts.csv:488:ma,11,ma-g11-ideas,MA:Cr1.1.III; MA:Cr2.1.III,ma-g10-ideas,ma-g12-studio,storyboard,storyboard,design principle; invention; framework; system; aesthetic; constraint,,"Pair design principles with idea methods to produce original ideas, then combine a developed aesthetic with knowledge of a system to propose an original framework under complex limits. What changes: systems knowledge and complex limits, where level 2 worked within resource limits. Assessed by a planning portfolio and prototype that frames a system under complex limits, scored on a rubric for range, research and a stated aim. Level 3 opens with a review unit of at most 6 days that serves as placement."
data/progressions/arts.csv:489:ma,11,ma-g11-make,MA:Cr3.1.IIIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIIb,ma-g10-make,ma-g12-studio,revision-log,revision-log,synthesize; hybridization; process; compelling; polish; viewer,,"Combine content and processes into one complex project with hybridization, and polish every element so it reaches its intended viewers and settings. What changes: hybrid, complex work polished throughout, where level 2 refined for chosen audiences. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:490:ma,11,ma-g11-craft,MA:Pr5.1.IIIa; MA:Pr5.1.IIIb; MA:Pr5.1.IIIc,ma-g10-craft,ma-g12-studio,production-roles,production-roles,master; frame a question; adapt; system; experimental; toolset,,"Manage and produce a media project with practiced skills, frame questions that guide a complex challenge, and adapt tools and styles across ordinary and experimental work. What changes: independent adaptation and questions framed by the student, where level 2 managed a production. Assessed by a production log and a tool-use task scored on a rubric for skill, risk and adapting. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:491:ma,11,ma-g11-present,MA:Pr4.1.III; MA:Pr6.1.IIIa; MA:Pr6.1.IIIb,ma-g10-present,ma-g12-exhibit,screening-plan,screening-plan,platform; promote; showing; impact; compare; global,,"Combine arts, media and school-subject content into one work that holds together across platforms, plan and promote showings aimed at a real effect, and compare the results. What changes: work that holds across platforms, and a showing aimed at real impact. Assessed by a finished cross-platform piece, a promotion plan and a written review comparing results, scored on a rubric for message, format and improvement. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
data/progressions/arts.csv:492:ma,11,ma-g11-read,MA:Re7.1.IIIa; MA:Re7.1.IIIb,ma-g10-read,ma-g12-criticism,look-describe-interpret,look-describe-interpret,synthesize; systems; persuade; top-quality; communicate; review,,"Bring together how parts relate across many kinds of pieces, and study top-quality examples to analyze how they steer, persuade and communicate across systems. What changes: systems and top-quality examples, where level 2 compared pieces for persuasion. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
data/progressions/arts.csv:493:ma,11,ma-g11-judge,MA:Re8.1.III; MA:Re9.1.III,ma-g10-judge,ma-g12-criticism,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,bias; complex context; rigorous; layered aims; feedback; independent,,"Critique the aims and impact of varied media while weighing setting and bias, and build a rigorous critique independently, seeking feedback on it. What changes: bias is weighed and feedback is sought by the student, unprompted. Assessed by a written critique that names its own bias check and records the feedback sought, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
