248:| 1 | K | Media all around us. *Where do we meet media in our day, and what do its parts tell us?* | 18 | ma-k-read; ma-k-society | MA:Re7.1.Ka; MA:Re7.1.Kb; MA:Cn11.1.Ka; MA:Cn11.1.Kb | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through the first viewfinder pictures (review). Works: Eadweard Muybridge, The Horse in Motion (United States, 1878, the Library of Congress, public domain), a row of photographs of one running horse; studio portraits by Seydou Keïta (Bamako, Mali, about 1949 to 1962), described and linked to a holding museum's page, never copied; Hildegard Westerkamp, Kits Beach Soundwalk (Vancouver, Canada, 1989; born 1946 in Germany; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a sound piece made on a walk by the sea, described and linked to the artist's own page; the class's own viewfinder drawings and sound map of media in the school (a sign, the bell, a poster, a screen), with no faces | MA:Re7.1.Ka: notice the parts of a media piece (pictures, sounds, words; still or moving) and tell what it seems to say, on Muybridge's row and on school signs. MA:Re7.1.Kb: notice and share how two pieces give different experiences (looking at the horse photographs against listening to the soundwalk with eyes closed). MA:Cn11.1.Ka: with help, share where media shows up in the child's day (the bell, a sign, a song, a picture book read aloud), on a media-hunt walk in the school, with a story character's day offered beside the child's own and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.Kb: with help, use media tools and spaces safely and politely: the viewfinder and any shared device held in two hands and set down on the table, a turn waited for, asking before pointing at anyone, no picture of a face. Technique: framing with a card viewfinder (hold, look, choose what is inside, draw it); a listening walk with a picture sound map. Words: media, part, message, sound, frame, safe. Showing: the class's "media in our school" wall of viewfinder drawings and sound maps. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.Ka, a talk item: on an unseen piece (an open-licensed photograph or a Curio Class sign card) the child points to two parts and says what it seems to say; MA:Re7.1.Kb, a talk item: an unseen still picture and an unseen short sound (a Curio Class original pair), how each feels to look at or listen to; MA:Cn11.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept "media in a day" drawing: one place media shows up in a day (the child's own or the story character's, marked the same), told to the teacher as the child draws; MA:Cn11.1.Kb, a table item: from the tray the child picks the viewfinder or the device and shows its safe, polite handling (two hands, set down, asks first, no face) and says one rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: making a piece that says something (unit 3), roles (unit 2). Tools: card viewfinder template, sound-map card, media-hunt picture cards, the media rules card, step cards, work cards, all Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed card. Free path: viewfinders cut by an adult from cereal-box card. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit needs the safe, polite tool habits and the looking and listening routine (the media-ethics-check and look-describe-interpret models start here). Looking and listening open the year so a child meets media before making it. Four ids, two techniques and the set-up and hand-back routine learned for the first time | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on device set-up and hand-back minutes inside a 40-minute session | Hazards and controls: media-hunt and listening walks: the class stays together and in sight; any shared device held in two hands, set on the table, wiped and handed back by the device card; screen breaks; viewfinder card edges checked smooth by an adult. No child is photographed or drawn from the viewfinder without a yes; the hunt points at things, never faces. Keïta photographed the people of Bamako in his studio