252:| 5 | 1 | Media that links people. *How do pictures and films link us to people we know, and how do two pieces feel different?* | 18 | ma-g1-read; ma-g1-society | MA:Re7.1.1a; MA:Re7.1.1b; MA:Cn11.1.1a; MA:Cn11.1.1b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Horace Poolaw (Kiowa, 1906 to 1984), photographs of Kiowa family and community life in and around Anadarko, Oklahoma (about 1925 to 1955), from the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian (its 2014 exhibition "For a Love of His People"), described and linked; Louis Lumière, Repas de bébé (Baby's Meal) (France, 1895, public domain), a family at breakfast filmed: a still and a moving picture of family life | Retrieval in day 2's opener: noticing parts and messages (ma-k-read) and safe, polite tool use (ma-k-society). MA:Re7.1.1a: name what media pieces contain (people, a place, things, a sound) and the messages they carry. MA:Re7.1.1b: with help, name how two pieces create different experiences (a still photograph of a family against a short film of one). MA:Cn11.1.1a: discuss media in daily life and how it links people you know (a picture sent to a grandparent, a call, a class newsletter), with a story character's or an invented family's link offered beside the child's own and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.1b: use tools and spaces by the rules, with safety and fair play: the class media rules the children help write (ask before a picture of anyone; a classmate only with their yes and the family's; the device shared by the turn card). Technique: far, middle and close shots with the viewfinder, drawn in three frames. Words: contents, message, far, close, link, fair. Showing: a "media links us" wall of three-frame drawings of places and objects. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.1a, a talk item: on an unseen open-licensed photograph, what it contains and its message; MA:Re7.1.1b, a talk item: an unseen still and an unseen short film clip of one subject, how each feels different; MA:Cn11.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept "media links us" drawing: a media link between people (the child's own, invented or a story character's, marked the same), told to the teacher; MA:Cn11.1.1b, a table item: with the viewfinder or the device from the tray, the child shows the turn card and the ask-first rule in action and names one class rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: media in cultural life (grade 2). Tools: the three-frame card, the class rules card, work cards, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: viewfinders from year K, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the tools and the rules after the summer and moves the looking routine from parts to contents and messages. Both kindergarten models (look-describe-interpret, media-ethics-check) are retrieved here, where they are first needed this year. The far, middle and close shots taught here are what unit 6's sketches in order draw | Candidate: a third photograph of family life by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked, for range | Poolaw photographed his own Kiowa family and community: the sensitivity agent studies the National Museum of the American Indian's pages and a Kiowa-run source first; the unit picks everyday-life photographs, not ceremonies, and no child imitates dress or regalia. Families vary: "people you know" includes classmates, neighbors and an invented family. Hazards and controls: shared devices by the turn card, wiped and handed back; screen breaks; no picture of a child without a yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
253:| 6 | 1 | Pictures in order. *How do media makers plan a piece in pictures, and what jobs does a team need?* | 22 | ma-g1-ideas; ma-g1-craft | MA:Cr1.1.1; MA:Cr2.1.1; MA:Pr5.1.1a; MA:Pr5.1.1b; MA:Pr5.1.1c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Émile Cohl, Fantasmagorie (France, 1908, public domain), a film made from hundreds of line drawings in order; Reiniger's Prince Achmed from year K, revisited: the same moving-picture idea made with cut paper instead of line | Retrieval in day 2's opener: play and a first plan (ma-k-ideas) and a tool used with a partner (ma-k-craft). MA:Cr1.1.1: show an idea in three sketches in order and a small model. MA:Cr2.1.1: with help, build a mockup of one named idea: a four-to-eight page flip book. MA:Pr5.1.1a: describe and show a role (planner, drawer, page holder, sound helper) and its skills (planning, tool steps, teamwork). MA:Pr5.1.1b: show basic creativity by changing up a technique (the flip book drawn again bigger, in color, or with a faster move). MA:Pr5.1.1c: try varied uses of a tool (the flip book flipped slow and fast, the viewfinder close and far) and show classmates what you found. Technique: the flip book, small changes from page to page. Words: sketch, order, mockup, flip book, role, try. Showing: team flip books shown to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept sketch page: three sketches in order and a small model; MA:Cr2.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept flip-book mockup: built from the named idea on the sketch page; MA:Pr5.1.1a, a talk item: with the role card, the child names the role and shows and tells one of its steps; MA:Pr5.1.1b, a guide look-for on the two kept flip books: the first version and the changed one; MA:Pr5.1.1c, a table item: with a flip book or viewfinder from the tray, the child shows one varied use and tells what it found. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: brainstorming many ideas (grade 2). Tools: the sketch-in-order card, flip-book template, role cards, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book. Free path: scrap paper stapled or clipped. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 5 because sketches in order draw its far, middle and close shots. Before unit 7, whose pattern and repetition need pages in order. Longest grade 1 unit: five ids, the flip book and the first named roles | Candidate: an animated work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked, for range | Team work: every role has a seated, no-drawing and no-speaking version; a child may work beside the team with a doubled task. Prince Achmed: as unit 2, only the flying horse and the craft are shown. Staples or clips by an adult; blunt scissors. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
254:| 7 | 1 | Patterns that repeat. *How do patterns and repeats help a media piece, and which part works best for its viewers?* | 10 | ma-g1-make; ma-g1-judge | MA:Cr3.1.1a; MA:Cr3.1.1b; MA:Re8.1.1; MA:Re9.1.1 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: J.D. 'Okhai Ojeikere, the Hairstyles series of photographs (Nigeria, from 1968), described and linked to a holding museum's page; Muybridge's The Horse in Motion from year K, revisited: a repeat of frames | MA:Cr3.1.1a: make and record content (frames drawn through the viewfinder, or photographs of objects on a shared device), assemble them into a six-frame strip, and spot pattern and repetition (a repeated frame, close, far, close). MA:Cr3.1.1b: try a change, notice what it does, and use it to finish. MA:Re8.1.1: with help, identify what different pieces mean. MA:Re9.1.1: name the part of a piece that works best and a possible change, thinking of its viewers. Technique: a six-frame sequence with a repeat, laid out as a strip. Words: pattern, repeat, record, effect, meaning, viewer. Showing: the strips shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept strip: six frames assembled with a repeat or pattern, which the child has circled on the last making day; MA:Cr3.1.1b, a guide look-for on the kept before-and-after card: one change tried and used to finish, and what it did, dictated or drawn; MA:Re8.1.1, a talk item: what an unseen piece means; MA:Re9.1.1, a talk item: the part of an unseen piece that works best and a change for its viewers. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: placing content to draw attention (grade 2). Tools: the six-frame strip card, the before-and-after card, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil frames on scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After unit 6 because a strip with a pattern needs pages in order and a team's roles; meaning and viewers build on year K's noticing. Short because the order, framing and roles are already built | Candidate: a pattern-based piece by a living maker from Asia or the Americas, described and linked | Ojeikere photographed Nigerian women's hairstyles: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and published accounts of the series first; the unit studies pattern in the photographs, and no child is asked about, photographed for or compared by their own hair or body (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
255:| 8 | 1 | A picture-book tale. *How can we mix a story, pictures and sound into one piece, and what do we bring to it from our own lives?* | 10 | ma-g1-present; ma-g1-sources | MA:Pr4.1.1; MA:Pr6.1.1a; MA:Pr6.1.1b; MA:Cn10.1.1a; MA:Cn10.1.1b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Yuyi Morales, Viva Frida (2014; Morales born 1968 in Xalapa, Mexico; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a picture book made from puppets she built and photographed in small sets, joined with painting, about the painter Frida Kahlo, described and linked to the maker's own page; kamishibai from year K, revisited: a tale told in pictures and voice | MA:Pr4.1.1: mix a subject, drawings and a sound into one piece: a picture-book tale on paper-stage cards or a four-page book, with a sound made at each page turn. MA:Pr6.1.1a: with help, talk about the showing conditions (where, the light, can everyone see and hear) and do one task in the showing. MA:Pr6.1.1b: with help, discuss how presenting felt. MA:Cn10.1.1a: draw on own experiences, interests and an example when making (Morales drew on her love of Kahlo's art; the child names an interest and an example of their own). MA:Cn10.1.1b: share a meaningful media experience. Technique: a sound made on cue at a page turn (voice, a found-object shaker, a tapped box). Words: mix, tale, sound, cue, conditions, example. Showing: the class picture-book tales, shown to families. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.1, a look-for at the showing (check day 1): subject, drawings and a sound joined; MA:Pr6.1.1a, a look-for at the showing: the child's one task done, with the kept screening-plan card made on the last making day naming two conditions; MA:Cn10.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept tale and its source card: an interest of the child's own and a named example; MA:Pr6.1.1b, a talk item (check day 2): how presenting felt; MA:Cn10.1.1b, a talk item (check day 2): a meaningful media experience, the child's own or the class-shared one, marked the same. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a unified narrated piece (grade 2). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source card, the sound-cue card, Curio Class's own. Free path: found objects for sound, scrap paper books. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in grade 1 because the tale joins the year's order, pattern and roles and ends the year after the year-end check (week 27); this unit's showing is its own assessment | Candidate: the sound-cue step tried with a quiet version (a visual cue card) for a child who is sensitive to noise | Kahlo: the unit tells her art, her garden and her animals as the book does; her illness and injury are named only as the book names them, and the sensitivity agent reads Morales's own published words on the book first. No child dresses as Kahlo or imitates her look (APPROACH: never by costume). Sound: shakers at low volume; hearing protection offered. Family yes for showing outside class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

