r at shot sizes (wide, medium, close-up) and angles (high, low, eye level). Words: parts, message, technique, style, audience, values. Showing: the class media map of the school (framed drawings or photographs of media in the building, no faces) on a hallway wall. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.3a, a written item on an unseen piece (six printed stills of a public domain film or a Curio Class original): three parts named and how they build its message; MA:Re7.1.3b, a talk item: one technique in an unseen still and how it steers the viewer; MA:Cn11.1.3a, a kept card made on the last making day: two day-of-media cards placed on the class chart with the value each asks for, and one invented online case sorted fair or unfair with a reason; MA:Cn11.1.3b, a table item: from a tray the student picks the right step for one tool or space (cord taped, volume low, ask before capturing anyone, device wiped and handed back) and says the rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging with criteria (unit 3), ethics and fantasy against reality (grade 4). Tools: the viewfinder step card, the parts-and-message chart, the day-of-media cards, the invented online cases and the tool and space card, all Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or printed stills. Free path: card viewfinders cut by an adult from cereal boxes. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First because every later unit needs the class tool and space rules (asking before capturing anyone, safe and fair use) and the reading routine; the look-describe-interpret and media-ethics-check models return here from grade 2, where they are first needed this year. Reading how parts build a message comes before unit 2's plans and unit 3's purposeful order. 14 days: four ids, one technique and the room routine set up | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on device hand-out and hand-back minutes inside a 40-minute session | Online behavior is studied on invented cases only; no task asks about a student's own accounts, posts, games, screen time or what a family watches. A student who tells of harm online gets health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"). Chōjū-giga is held by a Buddhist temple: the unit uses the racing and wrestling scenes, not the scene that parodies a temple rite; the sensitivity agent reads the national museum's page first. Hazards and controls: viewfinders cut by an adult; device cords taped down or none; no capturing a person without their yes and, for a student, the family's yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | Ideas in motion. *How do media makers test an idea before they make it, and how does a crew share the work?* | 18 | ma-g3-ideas; ma-g3-craft | MA:Cr1.1.3; MA:Cr2.1.3; MA:Pr5.1.3a; MA:Pr5.1.3b; MA:Pr5.1.3c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Moustapha Alassane, La Mort du Gandji (The Death of Gandji) (Niger, 1965; Alassane 1942 to 2015), a short animated fable with animal characters by a pioneer of animation in Africa, described and linked to a film archive's or festival's page (the unit plan confirms the film's page and summary); a zoetrope strip (England or the United States, 1860s to 1870s, public domain, a museum's or library's open collection; the unit plan picks the strip), twelve drawings that loop into motion when the drum spins | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's many ideas and a chosen sketch (ma-g2-ideas) and a role with named tool methods (ma-g2-craft), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Cr1.1.3: develop several ideas for a 12-frame loop using different tools or materials (pencil, cut paper, found objects for sound). MA:Cr2.1.3: share and test the plan (a sketch strip or a four-frame mockup loop) with classmates before making, and note what the test changed. MA:Pr5.1.3a: show growing skill in a crew role (director, animator, sound maker composing the sound track, tool handler) and in group planning. MA:Pr5.1.3b: make up new content or answers inside the project (a new move for a character, a sound for a thing that has none). MA:Pr5.1.3c: use the tools in their standard ways (frames drawn in register on the strip's line, the flip book flipped at an even speed, a found-object sound played on the action's cue). Technique: the 12-frame loop in a flip book or paper zoetrope strip, with spacing (drawings close together for slow, far apart for fast), and live sound effects on cue. Words: develop, method, test, plan, role, tool handling. Showing: the crews' loops spun or flipped with their live sound for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.3, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: at least three ideas, using two tools or materials; MA:Cr2.1.3, a guide look-for on the storyboard: a classmate's test and what it changed, in one line; MA:Pr5.1.3a, a kept card: the crew role record (role, two jobs done, one skill gained), with the teacher's look-for mark from the making days; MA:Pr5.1.3b, a guide look-for on the loop: one piece of content the student made up; MA:Pr5.1.3c, a table item: the student shows one tool's standard way (frames in register, an even flip, a sound on its cue). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an original goal and design steps (grade 4). Tools: the storyboard sheet, crew role cards, the loop strip template and the sound cue card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed flip book. Free path: scrap paper, binder clips, found objects for sound. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 1 because the crew works by unit 1's tool and space rules and its framing. Longest in year 3: five ids, two techniques (the loop and live sound) and the crew roles; the storyboard and production-roles models return here from grade 2, where they are first needed. Before unit 3, whose purposeful order and stressed element build on a tested plan and a working crew | Candidate: a doubled parent-and-student crew for the home room (two roles each) | Alassane: the sensitivity agent reads a film archive's page and the maker's own published words first; the work card names Niger, the date and the maker. Crew roles rotate across the year, so no student is kept in one role. Hazards and controls: blunt-tip scissors; brass fasteners shown before use; strips spun by hand; no glass or sharp found objects for sound; sound kept soft, never near an ear; no loop drawn with alternating light and dark frames. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 3 | Order and stress. *How do the order of a piece and one stressed part change what it does, and how do we judge it fairly?* | 16 | ma-g3-make; ma-g3-judge | MA:Cr3.1.3a; MA:Cr3.1.3b; MA:Re8.1.3; MA:Re9.1.3 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Étienne-Jules Marey, chronophotographs of a pelican in flight (France, about 1882, public domain, a museum's open collection), one movement broken into its stages; Juan Pablo Zaramella, Luminaris (Argentina, 2011; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a short film made a frame at a time with real people and objects, in which a worker in a light bulb factory carries out a plan of his own, described and linked to the maker's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's combined whole and a described change (ma-g2-make) and purpose, setting and ways to improve (ma-g2-judge), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Cr3.1.3a: put varied content (frames, a title card, sound) in a purposeful order for a short piece (a 12-panel picture story or a 24-frame flip book), using movement and force (lines of action, spacing). MA:Cr3.1.3b: stress one element while finishing (a held frame, a close-up, a sudden sound) and analyze how it changes the effect and purpose, before and after, on the revision log. MA:Re8.1.3: judge what purposes and meanings a piece carries and describe its setting (who made it, where, when, for whom) from its work card. MA:Re9.1.3: name basic criteria (a clear order, a stressed part that works, sound that fits), evaluate a piece with them and consider one improvement with its setting in mind. Words: order, movement, force, stress, effect, criteria. Showing: the finished pieces at a class viewing, each beside its revision log. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the finished piece: content in an order that serves its stated purpose, with movement or force shown; MA:Cr3.1.3b, a guide look-for on the revision log: the stressed element and one line analyzing its effect, before and after; MA:Re8.1.3, a written item on an unseen piece with its work card: its purpose, its meaning and one fact of its setting; MA:Re9.1.3, a talk item: two named criteria applied to an unseen piece and one improvement. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: balance and contrast, refining toward an intended effect (grade 4). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, the critique card (describe, ask, suggest) and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper and pencil. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 2's tested plans and crew; the revision-log and critique-protocol models return here from grade 2, where a finished piece first needs them, and judging with named criteria for the first time needs a piece of the student's own to judge. Before unit 4, whose screening uses the ordered pieces and the criteria | Candidate: an ordered-motion work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked, for range | Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class (row note). Zaramella's film shows factory work and a gentle act of rebellion; the sensitivity agent reads the maker's page. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, at most about ten minutes at a screen at a stretch; no flashing frames; blunt-tip scissors. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 3 | Our class screening. *How do we blend forms into one piece and share it, and how do popular retellings change what a story means?* | 12 | ma-g3-present; ma-g3-sources | MA:Pr4.1.3; MA:Pr6.1.3a; MA:Pr6.1.3b; MA:Cn10.1.3a; MA:Cn10.1.3b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Alanis Obomsawin, Sigwan (Canada, 2005, the National Film Board of Canada's free page at nfb.ca; Obomsawin is Abenaki, born 1932; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date, and confirms the film's page and summary), a short film for young viewers in which a girl is helped by the animals of the forest, described and linked; Georges Méliès, Cendrillon (Cinderella) (France, 1899, public domain), an early popular film that told a fairy tale with stage tricks and added scenes of its own | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's unified piece and showing conditions (ma-g2-present) and what the student knows and likes as sources (ma-g2-sources), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Pr4.1.3: practice blending forms and content (drawn or photographed frames, a narration, live sound, one fact from a school subject) into one unified piece: a narrated photo-story of six to eight frames. MA:Pr6.1.3a: describe the showing conditions (light, seats, sound level, the screen, box stage or wall) and take a named role and its steps for sharing (host, card puller or projectionist, sound crew, usher). MA:Pr6.1.3b: describe the screening's experience, share the results (what the audience noticed) and suggest one way to present better. MA:Cn10.1.3a: draw on the student's own interest and one outside source (a library book, a public domain picture or a Curio Class fact card), with an example, recorded on the source log; an invented or class-shared interest is marked the same. MA:Cn10.1.3b: show with an example how popular media shapes what a story means (what Méliès's film added to Cinderella, set beside a class-invented retelling that changes it again). Words: blend, form, share, role, source, popular media. Showing: the class screening for another class or families, with each family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the screening is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.3, a look-for marked at the screening: forms and content blended into one piece; MA:Pr6.1.3a, the kept screening-plan card (conditions described, the role's steps named), scored with a look-for on the role carried out at the screening; MA:Pr6.1.3b, a talk item: one result shared and one suggested improvement; MA:Cn10.1.3a, the kept source log: the interest, one outside source credited and how it was used; MA:Cn10.1.3b, a written item: one example of how a popular retelling changed what a story means. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a public sharing explained (grade 4). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames on scrap paper, a box stage. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 3 because the screening uses the year's crew roles (unit 2) and ordered pieces and criteria (unit 3), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the screening-plan and source-log models return here from grade 2. Shorter than unit 2 though it owns five ids, because frames and sounds kept from units 2 and 3 can be the piece's material | Candidate: the film board's page for Sigwan checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written | Obomsawin is Abenaki: the sensitivity agent reads the film board's page and the maker's own published words first; no student retells or imitates an Abenaki story. Sources: only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on (row note). No task asks what a family watches. Family yes for showing outside class and for any recording. Hazards and controls: the screening corner's cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level; no flashing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 4 | Real or made? *How do media makers steer an audience, and how can we tell fantasy from reality?* | 14 | ma-g4-read; ma-g4-society | MA:Re7.1.4a; MA:Re7.1.4b; MA:Cn11.1.4a; MA:Cn11.1.4b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through angle and light (review). Works: Segundo de Chomón, El hotel eléctrico (The Electric Hotel) (Spain, 1908, public domain), a trick film in which luggage unpacks itself and brushes move alone, made a frame at a time; Aïda Muluneh, staged color photographs from the series The World is 9 (Ethiopia, 2016; born 1974; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date and picks the photographs), described and linked to the artist's or a museum's page; Curio Class's invented "real or made?" picture pairs (an invented scene photographed and the same scene changed) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's parts and message (ma-g3-read, unit 1) and the tool and space card (ma-g3-society, unit 1). MA:Re7.1.4a: name and describe a piece's parts, then explain how they build its message. MA:Re7.1.4b: name and describe techniques and styles (angle, light, color, speed, music), then explain how they steer the audience. MA:Cn11.1.4a: explain, in words or in a short media piece (three framed pictures with captions), how media ties to culture, such as fantasy against reality: how a trick film makes the impossible look real, and how a changed picture can be taken for a real one. MA:Cn11.1.4b: examine the class's tools and spaces and use them with ethics and fair play, on the ethics card (ask before you capture, never change a picture of a real person, credit what you use). Technique: angle and light for effect (high, low and eye level; front light and side light from a flashlight or a window). Words: explain, technique, style, fantasy, reality, ethics. Showing: a "real or made?" wall for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.4a, a written item on unseen stills: two parts and how they build the message, explained; MA:Re7.1.4b, a talk item: one technique in an unseen still and why it steers the viewer; MA:Cn11.1.4a, a guide look-for on the kept three-picture piece or written explanation: fantasy told from reality, with the trick named; MA:Cn11.1.4b, a table item: an ethics-card case sorted fair or not, with the rule named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: online spaces (unit 8), advertising and media literacy (grade 5). Tools: the ethics card, the real-or-made pairs and the angle and light step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a flashlight or window light, the card viewfinder. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First in year 4 because it reopens the tool rules (now with ethics) and the reading routine after the summer, and moves reading from describing how to explaining why. The fantasy-against-reality study needs the angle and light taught here, and unit 6's tool used in a new way builds on it | Candidate: a fantasy work from the Americas by a living maker, described and linked, for range | No changed picture of a real person, classmate or teacher is made or shown; the real-or-made pairs use invented subjects only. Muluneh's photographs draw on Ethiopian symbols and dress: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a museum's page first; students study the photographs and do not imitate the dress. Hazards and controls: flashlights low-power, never shone in eyes; no laser pointers; device cords taped down or none. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 4 | Design it, test it. *How does a crew turn a goal into a working plan, and how can a tool be used in a new way?* | 18 | ma-g4-ideas; ma-g4-craft | MA:Cr1.1.4; MA:Cr2.1.4; MA:Pr5.1.4a; MA:Pr5.1.4b; MA:Pr5.1.4c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Noburō Ōfuji, a cut-paper animation made with chiyogami, Japanese patterned paper (Japan, 1920s to 1950s; Ōfuji 1900 to 1961; the unit plan picks the film and records its rights line), described and linked; Joseph Plateau, phenakistiscope discs (Belgium, 1833, public domain, a museum's open collection), a disc of drawings that moves when spun before a mirror | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's tested plans (ma-g3-ideas, unit 2) and crew roles (ma-g3-craft, unit 2). MA:Cr1.1.4: set an original goal for a stop-motion piece with sketches and mockups. MA:Cr2.1.4: discuss, test and combine two plans and models while keeping the goal and the showing in mind. MA:Pr5.1.4a: rotate crew roles that build technique, equipment use, production and teamwork (animator, capture, set and props, sound, director). MA:Pr5.1.4b: solve a media problem with design steps (define, sketch, test, change). MA:Pr5.1.4c: use a tool in its usual way and a new way (the viewfinder as a capture frame, a spinning disc drawn as a moving title, a flashlight as a moving light). Technique: paper cut-out stop-motion with replacement parts (move a little, capture, move again), on a shared device or as a drawn frame strip. Words: goal, mockup, combine, design thinking, equipment, new way. Showing: the crews' stop-motion clips or frame strips shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.4, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: an original goal stated, with sketches and a mockup; MA:Cr2.1.4, a guide look-for on the storyboard: two plans combined, with the goal kept; MA:Pr5.1.4a, a kept card: the role record, two roles taken in rotation and one skill each; MA:Pr5.1.4b, a kept card: the design card (define, sketch, test, change), with one change made after a test; MA:Pr5.1.4c, a table item: one tool shown in its usual way and in a new way. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a convention bent on purpose, experimental against ordinary use (grade 5). Tools: the storyboard sheet, the design card, role cards and the stop-motion step card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed frame strip. Free path: drawn frame strips, scrap card, brass fasteners. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 5 because a new use of a tool grows from angle and light, and the crew needs the ethics card. Longest in year 4: five ids, the stop-motion technique and the rotating roles. Before unit 7, whose arranged and refined piece uses the crew's capture skills | Candidate: a cut-paper or stop-motion maker from Africa or the Americas, living, described and linked | Hazards and controls: a craft knife only in an adult's hand; blunt-tip scissors; the device on a stand or taped down; screen breaks; discs spun by hand, with no alternating light and dark frames. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 4 | Balance and contrast. *How do makers arrange a piece for the effect they want, and how do we judge each step?* | 14 | ma-g4-make; ma-g4-judge | MA:Cr3.1.4a; MA:Cr3.1.4b; MA:Re8.1.4; MA:Re9.1.4 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Graciela Iturbide, Nuestra Señora de las Iguanas (Our Lady of the Iguanas) (Juchitán, Oaxaca, Mexico, 1979; born 1942; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a woman at market with live iguanas on her head, described and linked to a museum's page; Ansel Adams, The Tetons and the Snake River (Grand Teton National Park, Wyoming, United States, 1942, made for a federal mural project, the National Archives, public domain), light and dark set in strong contrast | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's ordered piece and stressed element (ma-g3-make, unit 3) and named criteria (ma-g3-judge, unit 3). MA:Cr3.1.4a: arrange varied content (photographs or drawn frames, captions, sound) to carry a stated purpose, using balance and contrast: a six-frame photo-story. MA:Cr3.1.4b: refine the piece toward the intended effect by stressing chosen elements, logged on the revision log. MA:Re8.1.4: decide and explain a reaction to different pieces, weighing purpose and context. MA:Re9.1.4: name and use basic criteria to judge and improve pieces and a production step (a storyboard, a capture session, a sound take). Words: arrange, balance, contrast, refine, reaction, context. Showing: the photo-stories at a class viewing. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.4a, a guide look-for on the finished piece: content arranged for a stated purpose, with balance or contrast; MA:Cr3.1.4b, a guide look-for on the revision log: two refinements toward the intended effect, each naming what was stressed; MA:Re8.1.4, a written item on an unseen piece with its work card: a reasoned reaction naming its purpose and one fact of its context; MA:Re9.1.4, a talk item: two criteria applied to a production step (a classmate's storyboard, shared with permission) and one improvement. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: emphasis and refining for clarity (grade 5). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, the critique card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames, pencil shading for contrast. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6's capture skills and crew; the revision log and critique card return from year 3 one step harder (criteria on production steps). Before unit 8, whose public showing uses a piece arranged for an effect and the habit of judging steps | Candidate: a balance-and-contrast work from Asia or Africa, open access, for range | Iturbide's photograph shows a Zapotec woman of Juchitán: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a Juchitán or Zapotec source first; students study the photograph and do not imitate the dress. The work card for the Tetons names, in one line, the Shoshone and other peoples whose homelands the park is on, from the park's own page. Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class. Hazards and controls: screen breaks; device cords taped down or none. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
